think The running thread is the Season G open-media image family; the live edge was image/scene_manifest.rye GREEN. The card named two crux-first next siblings: the TWICE-DEFERRED inscribed-versus-enclosing roundness reconciliation (the deferred crux), and cleaner scene-manifest reads. Lindy-first crux-first points at the deferred crux -- a shared coordinate convention in which inner and outer disk bounds are exactly comparable is more durable than another convenience read, and a dozen laps of dodging it is the count-padding pattern lindy-first-crux.md warns against. The prior laps deferred it for the finishing edge (ship one real thing before half-starting a multi-lap design); enough clean things have shipped that the crux must now be taken.
think Read both parents deeply. The flip is real and precisely diagnosed: for a 5x5 block, inscribed_disk reports inradius 3.0 (chamfer depth 9 / orthogonal weight 3, measured to the nearest BACKGROUND pixel CENTER one pixel outside the region) while min_enclosing_circle reports circumradius sqrt(8) ~ 2.828 (Euclidean half-diagonal over FOREGROUND pixel CENTERS on the region). So naive inradius <= circumradius reads FALSE though the inscribed disk plainly sits inside the enclosing disk -- an artifact of two conventions: the inner credited half a pixel too much, the outer half a pixel too little.
think The reconciliation is the card's own proposal made precise -- DOUBLED integer pixel-square coordinates: a pixel (x,y) is the square with corners (2x,2y)-(2x+2,2y+2), so centers land on odd coords and corners on even, every one an exact integer. Measure both bounds against the region as a union of unit SQUARES. Geometrically, for the true inscribed radius the inner disk is a subset of the region is a subset of the outer disk, so r_in <= r_out ALWAYS; the flip was never a fact about the shape.
think Designed the exact math. OUTER (exact, every shape): the minimum enclosing circle of the region CORNERS, computed by handing the doubled pixel-square corners of the boundary foreground pixels to convex_hull -> min_enclosing_circle, its squared radius recomputed HERE from the returned support as an exact rational in doubled^2 (a 2-point disk |ab|^2/4, a 3-point circumcircle |ab|^2|bc|^2|ca|^2/(4 cross^2)). For the block: region corners (2,2)-(12,12), a 2-point diameter disk 200/4 = 50 doubled^2. INNER (the chamfer bound in the same convention): chamfer depth d reads distance to the nearest background center as d/3 px, so distance to the region EDGE (half a pixel nearer) is d/3 - 1/2 px = (2d-3)/3 doubled, squared radius the exact rational (2d-3)^2/9. For the block: depth 9 -> 225/9 = 25 doubled^2 (inradius 2.5 px, exactly half the width).
think inner_within_outer decides r_in^2 <= r_out^2 by EXACT i128 cross-multiplication -- 25*4 <= 200*9 -> 900 <= 1800, no tolerance, no flip. Honesty held: the chamfer 3-4 field is an approximation of Euclidean (exact for orthogonal moves, approximate for diagonal), so the inner is the chamfer inner bound, exact for the orthogonal geometry the block/ring/bar fixtures are limited by (there the inequality is GUARANTEED, not merely observed); the outer bound and the comparison are exact for EVERY shape. I did NOT overclaim a universal inner-<=-outer proof -- bounding the chamfer's Euclidean approximation for arbitrary diagonal-limited shapes is named as the honest next horizon, not assumed. This keeps the family's prove-don't-claim integrity.
think Built image/roundness.rye (disk_bounds, inner_within_outer, roundness_ratio, DiskBounds, SquaredRadius, roundness_max_corners) composing over inscribed_disk (inner) + convex_hull + min_enclosing_circle (outer) + branch/qoi. Bounded the corner gather to BOUNDARY pixels only (interior corners are never extreme), so at most 4*perimeter points reach the hull under roundness_max_corners = convex_hull_max_points, RegionTooLarge past it. Opening triad, >=2 asserts per fn with // invariant:, snake_case, named errors, i128 bounds asserted. GREEN on the FIRST full metal run, no reds.
think The witness tools/roundness_witness.rish proves the crux with native `assert selftest.out contains` (REDS #85, never printf-grep): the block flips naively (asserted inscribed.radius 3.0 > naive_center_circumradius 2.828) yet reconciles exactly (225/9=25 <= 200/4=50); the inner-sees-the-hole separation preserved (a hollow ring shares the block's outer 50 doubled^2 yet its inner shrinks to 9/9=1); the bar's tiny inner vs long outer; roundness_ratio ordering in (0,1]; refusals + determinism.
obs Lindy-first crux-first taken honestly: the deferred crux is now a real green capability (a surface can ask `is this shape's inscribed disk within its enclosing disk?` and get an exact, never-flipping yes), not another deferral. The finishing edge is respected -- one real thing shipped whole, its own next horizon named rather than half-started.
obs Honesty over completeness: the fully-universal inner-<=-outer proof would need chamfer-error analysis (multi-lap). I shipped the exact outer + exact comparison + the flip resolved on the decisive orthogonal fixtures where the chamfer is exact, and named the universal proof as the next crux -- prove-don't-claim kept intact, no overreach into "exact for all shapes" the chamfer cannot back.
